## 2. Dark mode pipeline: Customizer to frontend

Site-wide **default** color mode belongs in the Customizer when that control is added. Visitors can still override via the existing header toggle (`localStorage` `mh-theme`).

```php
// Inside an App\Customizer registry, when Matt asks for this control:
$wp_customize->add_setting('sage_color_mode_preference', [
    'default' => 'system',
    'type' => 'theme_mod',
    'capability' => 'edit_theme_options',
    'sanitize_callback' => function ($value) {
        return in_array($value, ['system', 'light', 'dark'], true) ? $value : 'system';
    },
]);

$wp_customize->add_control('sage_color_mode_preference', [
    'label' => __('Default Visual Scheme Mode', 'sage'),
    'section' => 'sage_bespoke_brand_colors',
    'type' => 'select',
    'choices' => [
        'system' => __('Respect Operating System Mode', 'sage'),
        'light' => __('Force Light Layout', 'sage'),
        'dark' => __('Force Dark Layout', 'sage'),
    ],
]);
```

Apply the default in the root layout without dropping `language_attributes()` or the existing `mh-dark` class pipeline. Prefer:

```blade
<html @php(language_attributes()) @class(['mh-dark' => $isDarkDefault])>
```

Do not rewrite all `html.mh-dark` CSS to `[data-theme="dark"]` unless Matt asks to migrate. Tailwind v4 dark variants should keep targeting `.mh-dark` (for example `@custom-variant dark (&:where(.mh-dark, .mh-dark *));` in CSS), not a v3 `tailwind.config.js` `darkMode` key.

